{
  "teamId": 140,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 Texas Rangers entered the season as defending World Series champions, draped in the expectations that come with a title. But from nearly the first week, it was clear this team would struggle to recapture that magic. A thrilling Opening Series win, punctuated by Travis Jankowski's improbable home run and Jonah Heim's clutch single, gave Rangers fans reason to believe — but the cracks appeared almost immediately, as José Leclerc's blown save in April set the tone for a bullpen that would betray the team repeatedly throughout the year.\n\nThe spring months told a tale of a roster fighting above its weight class. Corey Seager's three-run blast in May and Jonah Heim's continued clutch hitting gave the team moments of genuine electricity, but the pitching staff hemorrhaged runs at a damaging rate. David Robertson surrendered a three-run homer to Minnesota in late May, and the rotation — led by a diminished Nathan Eovaldi — gave up grand slams in back-to-back weeks in late June. By the end of June, Texas was 38-46, buried deep in a division race they had no business contesting.\n\nJuly offered a fleeting resurrection. The Rangers went 14-11, their best month of the season, fueled by Marcus Semien's power surge and a pair of Jonah Heim clutch singles that salvaged games that looked lost. But the bullpen, particularly Kirby Yates and Jacob Latz, kept undercutting progress — giving back leads with agonizing regularity. August and early September brought the team's most emotionally charged stretch, with Josh Smith's walk-off two-run homer and Josh Jung's dramatic three-run shot in September providing rare highs amid persistent mediocrity.\n\nThe final weeks of the season became a bittersweet showcase for the future. Rookie Wyatt Langford announced himself with a September grand slam and a series of clutch knocks, while veterans like Semien and Seager still flashed the quality that made the 2023 title possible. Jonathan Ornelas's walk-off single in the season's final days provided a last moment of joy, but it could not obscure the truth: the Rangers finished 78-84, well short of the playoffs, their title defense undone by a porous pitching staff, an inconsistent bullpen, and an offense that ranked among the league's weakest. The dynasty window had not slammed shut, but it had closed considerably.",
